Title: "Defines"
Id: "13d7dffc-b893-4883-8713-94f043308359"

Text: "`Defines:` introduces a concrete typed value or operation. Its target is a
refined-capable declaration statement. A simple value must state its type with
`is` or a top-level build; a destructured component may inherit the known type
of the command value assigned to it.

```mlg-fragment
[CommandHeader]
Defines: <DeclarationStatement>
abstractly?:
using?: <DeclarationStatement>+
when?: <ClauseUnion>+
specifies?: <IsOrViaItem>+
expresses?: <ClauseUnion>+
Requires?: <RequiresItemUnion>+
Enables?: <EnablesItemUnion>+
Documented?: <DocumentedItemUnion>+
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
Id?: <OpenText>
```

```mlg-fragment
[\intersection:of{A}:and{B}]
Defines: I is \set
when: A, B is \set
expresses: I := \set@{x_ : x_ \"in\" A | x_ \"in\" B}
Documented:
. called: \"intersection of $A?$ and $B?$\"
```

A top-level build on the right of `:=` supplies the explicit type:

```mlg-fragment
[\empty.set]
Defines: E := \set@{x_ : ...}
Documented:
. called: \"empty set\"
```

For a destructured definition, every component needs exactly one concrete value,
through `:=` in `specifies:` or indirectly through `expresses:` for a callable
body. Assignment-backed `specifies:` facts are followed by type and capability
inference.

The zero-argument `abstractly:` marker deliberately leaves typed components
open for a later `Realizes:` item:

```mlg-fragment
[\naturals]
Defines: Nb ::= (N, 0, succ(n_))
abstractly:
specifies:
. N is \set
. 0 \"in\" N
. succ is \function:on{N}:to{N}
Documented:
. called: \"natural number system\"
```

Use `abstractly:` only when realizations are part of the design. A concrete
definition may not leave a target component without a value. `Documented:` must
contain `called:` or `written:`."
Id: "b2614ebb-ec03-4101-b137-fed2ef759073"
